<p>
Marcin Borkowski (mbork) <a href="https://mbork.pl/2026-10-17_Using_timers_to_perform_weekly_tasks">has an interesting problem</a> whose solution is harder than you think it would be. The problem is pretty simple: Every Sunday morning when he writes his weekly review, he needs to update all the clock tables in his journal. That usually takes about a minute and mbork had the idea that he could use an idle timer to run the update. Specifically, he reasoned that if Emacs were idle for a minute it probably meant that he was otherwise engaged and that Emacs would be idle for another minute so why not fire off his update?
</p>
<p>
Of course, he wanted to do this only once a week on Sundays so it was a bit more complex than just setting the idle timer. He came up with the idea of setting a (normal) timer for 3 AM on Sunday that would set the idle timer. That didn&rsquo;t quite work because of a technical issue but he got things working with yet another timer.
</p>
<p>
You should read his post for the details. His solution is interesting and shows that sometimes even simple things can be harder than we imagined.</p>
